quantum computing is getting quite a lot  of coverage these days in the media  which i think is fantastic but when I  read some of the things there are a few  things that are a bit wrong and I'm not  really criticizing anyone quantum  computing is very hard but I think  there's some room for some more nuance  so here are my top five clarifications  about quantum computing and standard  description of quantum computing  normally goes something like this  quantum computers are made of qubits  which can be in a state of 0 &amp; 1 at the  same time all of the qubits are then  entangled so they're all treated as a  single object that's in many different  states at the same time therefore a  quantum computer is like an infinitely  parallel computer and they're not quite  right so it's true that kuan computers  are in multiple states at the same time  before you measure them but as soon as  you measure them you only get one state  back parallel computing deals with  breaking down large problems into small  chunks which can each be solved on a  processor before the whole thing's  recombined but the architecture is very  different to quantum computers in a  parallel computer you've got many  different independent processes running  at the same time rather than an  entangled state and also you can read  out any of the states of any of the  processes at any time you like number 2  imagine this situation you've got  someone who says they've developed a  quantum algorithm that needs a hundred  qubits in order to run and you've got  another person who says I've built this  one computer and it's got hundred qubits  in it so any normal person would say ok  great you can just take that algorithm  and put it on that machine and get an  answer but in most cases that's really  not true and it's because they're  talking about two different kinds of  qubits in the algorithm sense what  they're talking about is an theoretical  ideal qubit which is commonly referred  to as a logical qubit whereas in a  quantum computer sense they're talking  about a real physical device which could  be like a loop of men  to an atom and electron photon something  like that and these physical qubits have  noise in them because they they're real  devices living in the real world and  noise is a killer for quantum algorithms  it will destroy your quantum state so  people are all trying to make qubits  with as low noise possible but the  quality of a qubit matters a huge amount  to how good your quantum computer is now  theoretically there's a way of getting  around noise in qubits using a technique  called quantum error correction which is  where you use multiple physical qubits  to simulate one logical qubit and how  many physical qubits you actually needs  to simulate a logical qubit depends on  how good quality those physical qubits  are the higher quality they are the few  you need the estimates range from say  10,000 physical qubits to a hundred  physical qubits per logical qubit that  brings me on to 0.3 which is quantum  computers and Internet encryption so a  lot of the internet encryption is based  on the fact that it's really hard to  factor large numbers on a classical  computer and this is quantum algorithm  called Shaw's algorithm that can  theoretically factor these large numbers  exponentially fast and the best  classical algorithm the trouble is for a  standard 128 bit encryption you'd need  about a thousand cubits for Shor's  algorithm to run and that means that  you'd need a million or more physical  qubits in order to run it and that's a  really massive number of qubits  currently we have the best is 72 qubits  that Google has in the universal quantum  computing scheme and so it's going to be  a very long time until we hit a million  so for the time being your internet  secrets are safe point number four is  the fact that nobody knows for sure that  con computing will ever work at scale  some people argue that noise is such a  significant issue that'll be impossible  to get say a million cubits all working  together without noise coming along and  ruining everything and it's a fair point  personally I'm  optimistic human ingenuity can go a very  long way some people said that we would  never detect gravitational waves on  earth because noise is a too significant  issue for that and yet the people at  LIGO did an absolutely incredible job  over many years and now we can detect  gravitational waves on earth which is an  amazing achievement it's not exactly  equivalent to quantum computing but you  know the only way we can find out  whether we can do it is by trying to do  it and like I say oh I'm optimistic but  it's always worth bearing in mind that  it's not guaranteed and the final point  I want to make is about quantum  supremacy now I think quantum supremacy  is a bad name because it poorly  describes the thing that it's trying to  describe I've done a whole video on  quantum supremacy so check that out if  you want to find more details about it  but basically it's the moment in time  where a quantum computer can do one  thing better than the best classical  supercomputers can do now currently  classical computers can do everything a  quantum computer can do and more but if  you think about quantum supremacy makes  it sound like it's the time when a  quantum computer can do everything a  classical computer can do and more but  that's really not what it is it's the  time when a quantum computer can do one  tiny little thing better than a  classical computer so it shouldn't  really be called quantum supremacy it  should be called something more like a  quantum glimmer of hope or a lot of  people are calling it a quantum  advantage which i think is probably a  better name well I hope that cleared up  a few things  gave you a bit more subtlety into what  Cantus can and can't do personally I'm  really excited about where quantum  computer is going to go in the future I  think the potential applications of it a  massive especially in the realm of  quantum simulation because that's  something that we really struggle to do  on classical computers because quantum  quantum systems are so hard to simulate  quantum computer can do it a lot better  theoretically and that could be  revolutionary in say like simulating  materials to find out what properties  different material  have with strength durability or exotic  things like high-temperature  superconductors also in chemistry if you  could simulate how molecules interact  say a molecule in a drug how that  interacts with many different  biomolecules in your body that would be  an amazing thing to do in something we  can't do right now so I'm very excited  to see what happens in the future I'll  keep you updated here thanks again to  the sponsor of this video brilliant org  what they've done is taken proper maths  and science and put it in this framework  where it pretty much feels like you're  solving puzzles but you're solving real  problems for math and science and  learning real STEM skills and when I'm  looking for a brain teaser I like doing  their problems of the week and the  problems range from being very  approachable through to some stuff that  isn't legitimately difficult they also  have courses on physics mathematics  computer science and they're adding more  content all the time and the thing I  love is when you're doing a hard problem  and you finally see how to solve it you  crack the problem and you get it right  and you have a moment of feeling like  yes I'm not complete idiot and that's  what I enjoy so if that sounds  interesting go to brilliant org slash do  s and I've also put a link in the  description below thanks for watching my  video and I will see you on the next one  [Music]
 

